tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [[92mINFO[0m] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>Design an educational video game aimed at teaching teenagers about financial literacy, including scenarios, gameplay mechanics, and reward systems to keep players engaged.<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>*leans forward eagerly*

Okay so imagine this: **"Broke to Stoked"**—you start as a college freshman with basically nothing except terrible decisions waiting to happen. I'm talking your buddy texts you about a "guaranteed" NFT investment at 2am and you actually have consequences if you YOLO your meal plan money into it.

*gestures wildly*

The core loop works like this: **Daily Dilemma** system where every in-game morning you get hit with financial decisions masquerading as normal life. Do you prep meals Sunday or blow thirty bucks daily on delivery? Subscribe to seventeen streaming services or... actually maybe don't? Your character's stress meter and social meter are constantly fighting each other—like yeah, that concert ticket drains your savings but skipping every event makes you isolated and depressed, which then makes you impulse-shop to feel better. *Vicious cycle, just like real life!*

And here's where I get devious with the **"Oops I Did It Again"** scenarios. Random events! Car breakdown. Medical bill. Your mom "borrowing" money she'll never repay. The game *wants* you to fail spectacularly so you learn why emergency funds exist. There's achievement badges for disasters—"Minimum Payment Cycle," "Overdraft Olympian," "FOMOruptcy"—because humiliation is educational.

*grins*

For the **rewards**... unlockable avatars? Nah, that's weak. You unlock *lifestyle tiers*. Studio apartment with roommates evolves into solo place with actual furniture, then maybe you get a plant that doesn't die because you can afford consistent groceries. The plant is a status symbol. Bragging rights.

The boss fights are credit card companies and predatory lenders with actual fine print you have to read. Beat them by understanding APR calculations under time pressure. *Evil, right?*

Want me to spiral into the stock market minigame mechanics or the "Tragedy of the Roommate Diner Check" social stealth sections?<|im_end|>
