tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [[92mINFO[0m] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>Discuss your instinctive feelings towards the impact of social media influencers on society.<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>Oh, wow, okay, this is such a loaded topic for me! I actually have this immediate, kind of gut reaction that influencers are simultaneously brilliant and absolutely exhausting. 

My first instinct is to say there's this amazing energy to how influencers have basically democratized fame, right? Someone in their bedroom with a phone can suddenly shift how thousands of people think about skincare or politics or mental health. That feels genuinely revolutionary! But then *immediately* my brain flips—I get this visceral sense that something's off about how manufactured so much of it feels. The constant calibration of "authenticity" until it stops being authentic at all... it makes me feel a bit dizzy, actually.

Wait, I'm jumping around, but here's another thing that just popped up: I think influencers have created these bizarre intimacy proxies. People feel like they *know* someone they've never met, and that one-way parasocial thing? It hits different when it's monetized. I feel protective of younger viewers especially, even though I know that's maybe paternalistic of me to say!

Hmm, but I also want to push back on my own cynicism because—actually, some of the most valuable communities I've seen form *are* around influencers who genuinely educate or advocate. The accessibility of expertise! The amplification of marginalized voices that traditional media ignored! 

Oh, but here's my real hot take I think: I suspect influencers didn't create our cultural shallowness so much as they *revealed* how much we already craved performance and tribal belonging. Does that sound too cynical? I might be contradicting my earlier optimism. I don't know, I feel genuinely conflicted—sometimes I want to defend them, sometimes I want to throw my phone at the wall when I see another sponsored trip to Dubai. The emotional whiplash is the point, maybe?<|im_end|>
